course: dictionary
lesson: 1774
title: Clew, clue and a ball of thread
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word «clew» has more than one meaning, and it is an old word. Today its main link is to «clue», because «clew» is an old spelling of «clue».
explain: «Clue» comes from «clew», a ball of thread; in a Greek myth the hero Theseus used a ball of thread to find his way out of a maze called the labyrinth. The thread led him out, so a clue now means something that leads you to an answer.
explain: In sailing, a clew is the lower back corner of a sail, where a rope is attached. To clew up a sail means to pull it up by its corners. Sailors use the word in a very different way from the myth, but both meanings are about a rope or thread that helps you.
fact: Clew is a noun and a verb. As a noun it can mean a corner of a sail or a ball of thread.
fact: Old-fashioned clew means a ball of thread or yarn.
fact: The old spelling «clew» was used for «clue» long ago.
fact: In sailing, «clew up» means to pull up a sail by its corners.
fact: Use the context to choose the right meaning: a maze means a thread, a boat means a sail.
exercise: In the Greek myth, what did Theseus use to find his way out of the labyrinth? | A pair of scissors | A map of the sea | A wooden torch | *A ball of thread
exercise: In sailing, what is a clew? | The front tip of the boat | A kind of anchor | *The lower back corner of a sail | The name of a sea monster
exercise: Which word today means something that leads you to an answer? | Sail | Labyrinth | Thread | *Clue
test: Which word is an old spelling of «clue»? | Clow | Clour | *Clew | Glue
test: What does «clew up» mean in sailing? | To fold the boat in half | To tie a knot in the rope | *To pull up a sail by its corners | To paint the sail blue
exam: Why does «clue» come from «clew»? | Because a clue is always a ball of thread | *Because the myth made the word up and it has no link to thread | Because a thread can lead you out of a maze, just as a clue leads you to an answer | Because a clew is always a sail
